TSDRPlugin_HackRF - HackRF One support for TempestSDR
=====================================================

This plugin gives TempestSDR access to the HackRF One (including a HackRF One
with a PortaPack attached, rebooted into "HackRF mode") via libhackrf
(hackrf.dll on Windows). It supports:

  * HackRF One (and HackRF Jawbreaker)
  * HackRF modes 2 (R9/full-duplex) and 6 (R8, single RX - the HackRF One)
  * macOS / Linux (via the system libhackrf library)

The capture is fixed to 8 MHz sample rate (matching the other TempestSDR
plugins); the centre frequency and gain are controlled from the main GUI in
the usual way.

PortaPack note
--------------

With a PortaPack board attached and running its own firmware, the device
enumerates as a PortaPack (CDC serial port) and is NOT visible to the plugin.
Before using TempestSDR with such a device, reboot it into HackRF mode: on the
PortaPack (Mayhem) firmware go to Settings/Setup -> "HackRF mode" -> enable and
reboot, or hold the rotary knob while powering on. The device will then show up
as a plain "HackRF One" (USB VID 1d50, PID 6089). To get back to PortaPack,
hold the knob while plugging it in again. A stock HackRF One without a
PortaPack needs no special handling.

Driver note (Windows)
---------------------

HackRF One normally uses Windows' built-in WinUSB driver (WCID) automatically
on Windows 10/11. If the device is not recognised, install/attach the WinUSB
driver with Zadig (https://zadig.akeo.ie): Options -> "List All Devices",
select "HackRF One" and press "Replace Driver" (or "Install Driver").

Building
--------

No Great Scott Gadgets SDK is required to build this plugin. On Windows the
API is loaded dynamically at runtime from hackrf.dll, so the plugin links
against nothing but the standard Windows system libraries. The matching
libhackrf ABI declarations are mirrored inside src/TSDRPlugin_HackRF.c
(libhackrf 2026.01.x). On Linux/macOS the plugin links against -lhackrf.

On Windows, build together with the Java GUI (from JavaGUI/):

    make all ARCHNAME=X64

The resulting TSDRPlugin_HackRF.dll is copied to JavaGUI/lib/WINDOWS/X64.

Runtime requirements (Windows)
-----------------------------

The plugin loads hackrf.dll (libhackrf, 64-bit, with libusb statically linked)
at runtime. The Java GUI packages hackrf.dll inside JTempestSDR.jar and, when
the "HackRF One" source is loaded, extracts it automatically into the same
temporary folder that holds TSDRPlugin_HackRF.dll (the Windows %TEMP% folder).
No manual copy is needed when launching the GUI from the jar, no matter which
folder it is launched from.

If for any reason the DLL is not found, the plugin checks, in order:

  1. the standard Windows library search path (the java.exe folder, System32,
     the current directory i.e. where TempestSDR was launched from, PATH),
  2. the folder that contains TSDRPlugin_HackRF.dll itself (i.e. %TEMP% when
     launched from the jar), and
  3. the folder named by the %TEMP% environment variable.

Manual placement only becomes necessary when running from class files without
the jar (development builds); in that case copy hackrf.dll (x64, same build as
the plugin) next to TSDRPlugin_HackRF.dll, into the launch folder, or into
System32.

The x64 hackrf.dll used for this build (libhackrf 2026.01.3, built with
MinGW-w64, libusb 1.0.27) is self-contained - it needs no other DLLs. If
hackrf.dll can not be found, loading the plugin fails with a clear message
pointing to this file.